"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_computer_science_en"][6]={"school_computer_science_en:M6-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M6-L1 – Classes, objects and interfaces<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Objects, algorithms and data structures<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Test cases and debugging<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish state, behaviour,\ninstances and interfaces in an object model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two rooms, one rule<\/h2>\n<p>Groups can sign up for two workrooms at the computing fair. Room 1\nhas two places and room 2 has one. Both rooms follow the same rule: if\nno place is free, another booking is rejected. The rooms should respond\nindependently. When someone books a place in room 1, the availability in\nroom 2 stays the same. How can we write the shared rule once and use it\nfor both rooms?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Class, instance and interface<\/h2>\n<p>In this model, a <strong>class<\/strong> describes the data and\noperations involved in booking a room. A concrete\n<strong>object<\/strong>, also called an <strong>instance<\/strong> of the\nclass, has its own values. Its <strong>state<\/strong> comprises the\ncapacity and the number of places already booked. Its\n<strong>behaviour<\/strong> consists of the supported operations used to\ninspect or change that state.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>interface<\/strong> is the agreement that calling code can\nrely on. Reading <code>free<\/code> gives the number of free places.\n<code>reserve()<\/code> returns <code>True<\/code> and books one free\nplace or returns <code>False<\/code> and leaves the state unchanged. The\ninternal representation can change as long as the promised behaviour is\npreserved.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_022_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Room creates room 1 with two places and room 2 with one. After booking one place in room 1, one place is booked there and none in room 2.\" />\n<figcaption>The operation follows the same rule and changes only the\nstate of the instance it is called on.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>An <strong>invariant<\/strong> is a condition that holds after the\nobject has been created and after each operation has finished. Here the\nnumber of booked places always stays between zero and the room’s\ncapacity. <strong>Encapsulation<\/strong> here means arranging for the\nnumber of bookings to change through the defined operation. That lets us\ncheck the rule in one place.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Run the state transition<\/h2>\n<p><code>Room(2)<\/code> creates a new instance and passes the capacity\n<code>2<\/code> to <code>__init__<\/code>. Within a method, Python\nassociates <code>self<\/code> with the particular instance.\n<code>@property<\/code> lets callers read <code>free<\/code> like a value\neven though it is calculated from two stored values.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> Room:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">__init__<\/span>(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, capacity):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(capacity) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> capacity <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;capacity must be a positive integer&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>._capacity <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> capacity<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>._booked <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"at\">@property<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> free(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>._capacity <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>._booked<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> reserve(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.free <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>._booked <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>first <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> Room(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>second <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> Room(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (first.free, second.free) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> first.reserve() <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (first.free, second.free) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> first.reserve() <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (first.free, second.free) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> first.reserve() <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">False<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (first.free, second.free) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Before the first booking, room 1 has zero booked places. The first\ntwo calls raise that count to one and two. On the third call,\n<code>free<\/code> is zero, so the method returns before the assignment.\nRoom 2 has zero booked places throughout.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use the two rooms in their final state from the example and call\n<code>second.reserve()<\/code> twice. Give the return values and the\nnumber of free places.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Design an interface for cancelling a booking. What precondition does\na successful cancellation require to preserve the invariant?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">After <code>alias = first<\/code>, a place is cancelled through\n<code>first<\/code>. What must <code>alias.free<\/code> show afterwards if\nthe cancellation succeeds?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The first call returns <code>True<\/code> and the second returns\n<code>False<\/code>. Both rooms then have zero free places. Only the\nfirst call changes room 2.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>cancel()<\/code> can return <code>True<\/code> and decrease the\nnumber of booked places by one if at least one place is booked. If there\nare zero bookings, the method returns <code>False<\/code> and leaves the\nstate as it is.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>alias.free<\/code> shows one free place. The assignment creates\nno second instance and both names refer to the same object.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mistakes and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The underscore in <code>_booked<\/code> signals internal use in\nPython. Assignment from outside the class remains possible and can break\nthe invariant. The example assumes that calls occur one after another.\nWith simultaneous requests, checking and changing the count require\nsuitable synchronisation. Two numbers are also insufficient to represent\nnamed bookings or a schedule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>A class brings shared rules together and each new instance has its\nown state. A testable interface specifies return values and effects. For\neach state-changing method, test the successful case and at least one\nedge case in which the state should stay unchanged.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2 – Linear and binary\nsearch<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.2 · Distinguish state,\nbehaviour, instances and interfaces in an object model. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.13/tutorial/classes.html\">Python\nSoftware Foundation – Python Tutorial – Classes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference:\n§9.3 · Distinguish state, behaviour, instances and interfaces in an\nobject model. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified:\n2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M6-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M6-L2 – Linear and binary search<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Objects, algorithms and data structures<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L5 – Test cases and debugging<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare search invariants and\npreconditions and count logical steps.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Which entries must be checked?<\/h2>\n<p>A list records the numbers of material boxes that have been handed\nin. A particular number may be present or missing. Without a guarantee\nof order, we read the entries from the start and stop at the first\nmatch. When the list is sorted in ascending order, checking its middle\ncan rule out a large portion of the remaining positions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two search algorithms and their invariants<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Linear search<\/strong> checks one entry after another. Before\nchecking position <code>i<\/code>, the target has not appeared at any\nearlier position. Reaching the end therefore establishes that the target\nis absent. This <strong>invariant<\/strong>, a statement that holds again\nat each check, requires no sorting.<\/p>\n<p>Our <strong>binary search<\/strong> uses a closed search interval from\n<code>lo<\/code> to <code>hi<\/code>, including both endpoints. Before\neach step, if there is a match anywhere, at least one match lies in this\ninterval. When the middle value is smaller than the target, we can rule\nout the midpoint and every position to its left. When it is larger, we\nrule out the midpoint and the right-hand portion. This reasoning\nrequires the values to be sorted in ascending order. When\n<code>lo &gt; hi<\/code>, the interval is empty.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_023_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"For target 41, linear search checks indices 0 through 6. Binary search visits intervals 0 through 7, 4 through 7 and 6 through 7, checking midpoints 3, 5 and 6 respectively.\" />\n<figcaption>The count is the number of positions each algorithm inspects\nfor target 41.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The example counts <strong>list positions inspected<\/strong>. A step\ncan use more than one comparison operator in Python. For lists that\nsupport direct access by index, the worst-case number of steps grows\nlinearly or logarithmically with length <code>n<\/code>, respectively. We\nwrite this as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLinear search may need every entry. After an unsuccessful binary search\nstep, at most about half the candidates remain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Trace a match and a miss<\/h2>\n<p>The list <code>[3, 7, 12, 18, 24, 29, 41, 56]<\/code> has indices 0 to\n7. To find 41, linear search reads the seven positions from 0 through 6.\nBinary search follows these intervals:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interval<\/th>\n<th>Midpoint<\/th>\n<th>Value<\/th>\n<th>Result<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0–7<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>18<\/td>\n<td>Continue in 4–7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4–7<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>29<\/td>\n<td>Continue in 6–7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6–7<\/td>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>41<\/td>\n<td>Match<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For a target of 20, binary search checks 18, 29 and 24. After the\nfinal check, the interval from 4 to 3 is empty. Both functions return an\nindex on success or <code>None<\/code> otherwise, together with the\nnumber of positions inspected.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> linear_search(values, goal):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i, value <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">enumerate<\/span>(values):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> value <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> goal:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> i, i <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(values)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> binary_search(values, goal):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    lo, hi <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(values) <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    probes <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> lo <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> hi:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        mid <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (lo <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> hi) <span class=\"op\">//<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        value <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> values[mid]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        probes <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> value <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> goal:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> mid, probes<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> value <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> goal:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            lo <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mid <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            hi <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mid <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, probes<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>values <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">18<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">29<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">41<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">56<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> linear_search(values, <span class=\"dv\">41<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> binary_search(values, <span class=\"dv\">41<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> linear_search(values, <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> binary_search(values, <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> binary_search([], <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Search for 56 using binary search. Give the values inspected and the\nreturn value.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Use <code>[2, 9, 4]<\/code> and target 4 to show why binary search\nneeds its precondition.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do the guarantees of these implementations differ when the\ntarget occurs more than once?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The values inspected are 18, 29, 41 and 56. The return value is\n<code>(7, 4)<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The midpoint value 9 causes binary search to discard the right-hand\nportion containing the match. After inspecting 2, binary search returns\n<code>(None, 2)<\/code>. Linear search returns <code>(2, 3)<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Linear search returns the first matching index. The binary search\nshown returns any matching index, depending on which midpoints are\nselected.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mistakes and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>After a miss at the midpoint, exclude that position. Using\n<code>lo = mid<\/code> could leave the upper half the same size, for\nexample when there is only one candidate. Sorting an unsorted list first\nrequires extra work and can change the original position numbers. Index\n<code>0<\/code> is a valid match. Use <code>index is not None<\/code> to\ntest whether a match was found.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Linear search works without ordering. Binary search relies on sorted\ninput and direct positional access, and shrinks its interval after every\nunsuccessful check. To compare the algorithms in an experiment, state\nthe target, the return contract and what you are counting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3 – Sorting, correctness and\ncost<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.2 · Compare search\ninvariants and preconditions and count logical steps. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.berlin.de/sen/bildung/unterricht/faecher-rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplaene/rahmenlehrplan-informatik-go-teil-c.pdf?ts=1705017673\">Senatsverwaltung\nfür Bildung, Jugend und Familie Berlin – Rahmenlehrplan für die\ngymnasiale Oberstufe – Informatik, Teil C<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §4 ·\nCompare search invariants and preconditions and count logical steps. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M6-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M6-L3 – Sorting, correctness and cost<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Objects, algorithms and data structures<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2 – Linear and binary search<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Trace insertion sort and justify\nits quadratic worst-case cost.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Put an unordered sequence in order<\/h2>\n<p>When filing index cards, you can take a card from an unsorted pile\nand put it in its place among the cards that are already in order. A\nlist can be sorted using the same idea. In <strong>insertion\nsort<\/strong>, the ordered prefix grows from left to right.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The sorted prefix<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>prefix<\/strong> is a contiguous initial part of a list.\nJust before round <code>i<\/code>, the prefix at positions <code>0<\/code>\nthrough <code>i - 1<\/code> is sorted in ascending order and contains\nexactly the elements processed so far. This is the loop invariant.\nBefore the first round that prefix contains one position. A single\nelement is already sorted.<\/p>\n<p>Each round saves the new value at position <code>i<\/code>\ntemporarily. Larger values in the prefix are shifted one position to the\nright, working from right to left. The saved value goes into the\nresulting gap. Since all values remaining farther to the left are no\nlarger than the new value, the extended prefix is sorted. Keeping the\nvalue in a separate variable prevents it from being lost as elements\nmove. After the final round, the prefix covers the entire list. This\nestablishes both the order and the preservation of the elements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_024_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"The prefix grows from 7 to 3 and 7, then to 3, 5 and 7. After the final step, the full list is 2, 3, 5 and 7.\" />\n<figcaption>Each round moves one value from the remainder to its proper\nplace in the prefix.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The number of shifts depends on the input. When the list is already\nsorted, each round checks the immediate neighbour and shifts nothing.\nWith a strictly descending input, round <code>i<\/code> must shift all\n<code>i<\/code> previous values. In a list of <code>n<\/code> entries,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1 + 2 + \\cdots + (n-1) = n(n-1)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshifts. Worst-case growth is quadratic, or\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Insert values and count the steps<\/h2>\n<p>For <code>[7, 3, 5, 2]<\/code>, the sorted portion starts with\n<code>[7]<\/code>. The rest of the sort proceeds as follows:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>New value<\/th>\n<th>Prefix afterwards<\/th>\n<th>Shifts<\/th>\n<th>Value comparisons<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>[3, 7]<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>[3, 5, 7]<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>[2, 3, 5, 7]<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>When inserting 5, the algorithm first shifts 7. The second value\ncomparison, against 3, ends the search for the insertion position. We\ncount only comparisons between list values and leave index checks out of\nthe count. The Python example works on a copy of the supplied list.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> insertion_sort(values):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    items <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> values.copy()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    comparisons <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> shifts <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(items)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        value <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> items[i]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        j <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> i<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> j <span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            comparisons <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> items[j <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> value:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            items[j] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> items[j <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            shifts <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            j <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        items[j] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> value<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> items, shifts, comparisons<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>values <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> insertion_sort(values) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>], <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> values <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> insertion_sort([]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ([], <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> insertion_sort([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>], <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> insertion_sort([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>], <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sort <code>[4, 1, 3]<\/code>. Give both new prefixes and the total\nnumbers of shifts and value comparisons.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the result contain exactly the same elements as the\noriginal list? Why would checking only that the result is in ascending\norder be insufficient?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two labelled cards A and B have the same sort key of 5. A appears\nfirst. What does the stopping condition mean for their relative\norder?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The prefixes are <code>[1, 4]<\/code> and <code>[1, 3, 4]<\/code>.\nEach round needs one shift, and the rounds use 1 and 2 comparisons\nrespectively. The totals are 2 shifts and 3 comparisons.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Each round saves the current element, shifts other elements into the\ngap and puts the saved element back. A sequence such as\n<code>[1, 1, 1]<\/code> is in order but might still contain the wrong\nelements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A remains before B. The algorithm stops shifting when it encounters\nan equal value. Preserving the relative order of equal keys is called\n<strong>stability<\/strong>. An implementation for labelled cards would\nneed to compare their keys.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mistakes and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Comparisons and shifts contribute to cost in different ways. Here a\nshift means an assignment from the sorted prefix. Placing the saved\nvalue is a separate assignment and is excluded from this count. Because\nthe function makes a copy at the start, it uses extra memory for the\nresult list.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Insertion sort preserves a sorted prefix and extends it one item at a\ntime. The invariant establishes correctness and the sum of shifts in the\nworst case explains the quadratic cost. The sorted list can then be used\nfor <a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">binary search<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4 – Recursion, stacks and\nqueues<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.2 · Trace insertion sort\nand justify its quadratic worst-case cost. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildung.rlp.de/fileadmin/user_upload/informatik.bildung.rlp.de/Lehrplaene/Informatik_LP_SekII.pdf\">Rheinland-Pfalz,\nLehrplan Informatik für Grund- und Leistungsfach in der Mainzer\nStudienstufe – Lehrplan Informatik – Grund- und Leistungsfach,\ngymnasiale Oberstufe<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.4, S. 54–55 · Trace\ninsertion sort and justify its quadratic worst-case cost. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M6-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M6-L4 – Recursion, stacks and queues<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Objects, algorithms and data structures<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (basic level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L3 – Sorting, correctness and\ncost<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Connect base cases and recursive\nprogress with LIFO and FIFO interfaces.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Make the problem smaller one step at a time<\/h2>\n<p>How many different orders are possible for three different robot\nmodels? There are three choices for the first place. After making that\nchoice, two models remain and their orders can be counted in the same\nway. Each choice leaves a smaller task. The question is which input lets\nus give an immediate answer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursion, stacks and queues<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>recursion<\/strong>, a function calls itself on a smaller\nsubproblem. The <strong>base case<\/strong> returns a result directly.\nWith valid inputs, the recursive step must make progress towards the\nbase case. For the number of possible orders, the rule is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n! = n \\cdot (n-1)!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThere is exactly one way to arrange the empty sequence, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>!<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0!=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A call that has not finished retains its local values and the point\nat which it should resume. To trace calls, we can represent those\nrecords on a <strong>call stack<\/strong>. The most recent call finishes\nfirst and is removed. This is LIFO (<em>last in, first out<\/em>). A\n<strong>queue<\/strong> removes the entry that has been waiting the\nlongest first. This is FIFO (<em>first in, first out<\/em>).<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_025_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"The calls start at 3 and continue through 2 and 1 down to 0. Then the stack returns 1, 1, 2 and 6 from top to bottom.\" />\n<figcaption>The base case is at the top of the call stack and returns\nfirst.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>An <strong>abstract data type<\/strong> specifies the operations\noffered and their guarantees independently of how items are stored. A\nstack, for example, adds and removes at the same end. A FIFO queue adds\nat the back and removes from the front. A Python list can implement a\nstack. With a list as a queue, removing the front item can require\nmoving many entries. Python supplies <code>collections.deque<\/code> for\nfrequent removals at either end.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Trace calls and returns<\/h2>\n<p><code>factorial(3)<\/code> waits for <code>factorial(2)<\/code>, that\ncall waits for <code>factorial(1)<\/code>, and that call waits for\n<code>factorial(0)<\/code>. From bottom to top, the stack then holds\narguments 3, 2, 1 and 0. The top call returns 1. The waiting calls then\nreturn\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\cdot1=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot1=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot2=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nin that order.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> collections <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> deque<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> factorial(n):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(n) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> n <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;n must be a nonnegative integer&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> n <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> n <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> factorial(n <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> factorial(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> factorial(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> factorial(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>stack <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>stack.append(<span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> stack.pop() <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> stack <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>queue <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> deque([<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>queue.append(<span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> queue.popleft() <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(queue) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Both collections start with A before B and accept C. The stack then\nremoves C. The queue removes A. The top of the call stack is the end\nwhere new calls are added.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Write down the call arguments for <code>factorial(2)<\/code> and the\nreturned values in chronological order.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A and B arrive in an empty queue. Remove one job. C then arrives and\na further job is removed. Give the two jobs removed and the one that\nremains. Repeat with a stack.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An incorrect function makes the recursive call\n<code>factorial(n + 1)<\/code>. Why does a correctly written base case\nfail to help with positive starting values?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The call arguments are 2, 1, 0. The returns are 1 from the call with\n0, 1 from the call with 1, and 2 from the call with 2, in that\norder.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">FIFO removes A and B. C remains. LIFO removes B first and C after it\nhas been added. A remains.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The argument increases instead of moving down towards zero. The base\ncase is never reached. In Python the call eventually fails at the\nrecursion depth limit.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mistakes and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Even correct recursion can hit Python’s recursion depth limit on\nlarge inputs. A loop with a running product can also compute a\nfactorial. Large integers can take time and memory even in the loop\nversion. An interface for either a stack or a queue must also specify\nwhat happens when someone tries to remove an item from an empty\ncollection. Here <code>pop()<\/code> and <code>popleft()<\/code> raise\n<code>IndexError<\/code>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>For recursion, check the base case, progress towards it and the way\nthe partial result is combined. On the call stack, the most recent call\nfinishes first. A FIFO queue removes the oldest waiting entry. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">breadth-first search<\/a>, this ordering\ndetermines which vertices are examined next.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L5 – Trees, graphs and\nbreadth-first search<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.2 · Connect base cases and\nrecursive progress with LIFO and FIFO interfaces. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/12/informatik/grundlegend\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 12 – Informatik, grundlegendes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: Inf12 LB1–3 · Connect base cases and recursive\nprogress with LIFO and FIFO interfaces. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildung.rlp.de/fileadmin/user_upload/informatik.bildung.rlp.de/Lehrplaene/Informatik_LP_SekII.pdf\">Rheinland-Pfalz,\nLehrplan Informatik für Grund- und Leistungsfach in der Mainzer\nStudienstufe – Lehrplan Informatik – Grund- und Leistungsfach,\ngymnasiale Oberstufe<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.4 · Connect base cases\nand recursive progress with LIFO and FIFO interfaces. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_computer_science_en:M6-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M6-L5 – Trees, graphs and breadth-first search<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M6 – Objects, algorithms and data structures<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Upper secondary (advanced level)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prior knowledge:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L4 – Recursion, stacks and\nqueues<\/a><\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Represent graphs and explain\nshortest unweighted paths using breadth-first search.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Find a route with few steps<\/h2>\n<p>A map of the school grounds shows locations and direct footpaths. We\nwant to travel from entrance A to room F in as few segments as possible.\nA search can inspect A’s immediate neighbours before reaching places two\nor more steps away. The map needs no distances because the task counts\ntransitions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graphs, trees and distance levels<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>graph<\/strong> consists of <strong>vertices<\/strong>, here\nlocations, and <strong>edges<\/strong>, here connections. If every path\ncan be used in both directions, the graph is undirected. An\n<strong>adjacency list<\/strong> records which other vertices are\ndirectly accessible from each vertex. In an undirected representation,\neach connection is recorded at both endpoints.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>tree<\/strong> is a connected undirected graph with no\ncycle. Between any two vertices, it has exactly one simple path, which\nrepeats no vertex. In <strong>breadth-first search<\/strong>, a general\ngraph gives rise to a tree containing the vertices reachable from the\nstart. Each newly discovered vertex gets exactly one predecessor, called\nits <strong>parent<\/strong>. The original graph can have extra edges and\ncycles.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-computer-science-en/media/img/S_COS_026_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A is at level 0, B and C at level 1, D and E at level 2, and F at level 3. Solid edges join A with B and C, B with D, C with E, and D with F. Extra edges C–D and E–F are dashed. G is unreachable.\" />\n<figcaption>The solid parent edges form the search tree for the chosen\nadjacency order.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The search stores discovered vertices in a FIFO queue. Starting\nvertex A opens level 0. When the search removes a vertex at level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit marks its undiscovered neighbours and adds them at the back as level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey wait behind any remaining vertices at level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach level is therefore fully processed before the next one gets its\nturn. A vertex first reached at level\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas a path with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nedges via its parent. If a shorter path existed, its penultimate vertex\nwould have triggered the discovery from an earlier level. The level\ntherefore gives the smallest number of edges from the start.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Derive the visit order and a path<\/h2>\n<p>Neighbours are examined in the order in which they appear in the\nlists. New vertices are marked <strong>when they are added<\/strong>.\nThis keeps D from entering the queue twice even though both B and C\nconnect to it.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Removed<\/th>\n<th>Newly discovered<\/th>\n<th>Queue afterwards<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td>B, C<\/td>\n<td>B, C<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td>D<\/td>\n<td>C, D<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td>D, E<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D<\/td>\n<td>F<\/td>\n<td>E, F<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td>none<\/td>\n<td>F<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F<\/td>\n<td>none<\/td>\n<td>empty<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> collections <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> deque<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> breadth_first(graph, start):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    parents <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {start: <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    order <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    queue <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> deque([start])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> queue:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        node <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> queue.popleft()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        order.append(node)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> neighbor <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> graph[node]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> neighbor <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> parents:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                parents[neighbor] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> node<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                queue.append(neighbor)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> parents, order<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> path_to(parents, target):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> target <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> parents:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    path <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> target <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        path.append(target)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        target <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> parents[target]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> path[::<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>graph <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;D&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;D&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;D&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;F&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;F&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;F&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;D&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"st\">&quot;G&quot;<\/span>: [],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>parents, order <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> breadth_first(graph, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> order <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;C&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;D&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;E&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;F&quot;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> path_to(parents, <span class=\"st\">&quot;F&quot;<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;D&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;F&quot;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> path_to(parents, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> path_to(parents, <span class=\"st\">&quot;G&quot;<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>From F, the parents lead backwards to D, B and A. Reversing this\norder gives A–B–D–F with three edges. G is a single vertex with no\nconnections to the rest of the graph. It does not appear in the results\nof the search from A.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Start at B. Give the removal order and the path found from B to\nF.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Reverse the order of B and C only in A’s adjacency list. Which path\nto F results, and what stays the same?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What extra information could make the shortest route from A to F\ndifferent from the route with the fewest edges?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The order is B, A, D, C, F, E. B adds D directly and D later\ndiscovers F. The path B–D–F has two edges.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A now adds C before B. C discovers D ahead of B and also discovers E\nin the same step. D is removed before E and discovers F first. The path\nA–C–D–F still has three edges.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If path lengths or travel times are assigned as edge weights, a path\nwith more edges can have a shorter total distance or travel time.\nUnweighted breadth-first search guarantees only the minimum number of\nedges in that case.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mistakes and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Marking a vertex only at removal can cause duplicate entries in the\nqueue. The immediate entry in <code>parents<\/code> prevents this even\nwhen cycles exist. The implementation expects both the start and every\nlisted neighbour to exist as keys. Vertex labels are strings.\n<code>None<\/code> is reserved for the start vertex’s missing parent.\nWith adjacency lists and fast dictionary access on average, we can\naccount for both time and storage: every reachable vertex is visited\nonce and every associated adjacency entry is examined once. Thus the\ntime is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(|V|+|E|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor reachable vertices and edges and the additional storage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(|V|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nexcluding the input and reconstruction of further paths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary<\/h2>\n<p>Breadth-first search inspects reachable vertices in increasing order\nof distance measured in edges. Marking vertices when they join the queue\nprevents duplicate visits. Parents form a tree from which we can read a\npath with the fewest edges. If paths of equal length exist, the\nparticular path found can depend on adjacency order.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"further-reading-internal\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_computer-science&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L1 – Propositional logic and\ndigital circuits<\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3 id=\"sources-external\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://informatikstandards.de/fileadmin/GI/Projekte/Informatikstandards/Dokumente/Bildungsstandards_SII.pdf\">Gesellschaft\nfür Informatik e. V. – Bildungsstandards Informatik für die\nSekundarstufe II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference: §3.2.2 · Represent graphs and\nexplain shortest unweighted paths using breadth-first search. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/11/informatik/ntg\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 11 – Informatik (NTG)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lesson reference:\nInf11 LB1 (BFS mit Adjazenzmatrix) · Represent graphs and explain\nshortest unweighted paths using breadth-first search. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-22 · Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.lehrplanplus.bayern.de/fachlehrplan/gymnasium/12/informatik/grundlegend\">Bayern\n– Staatsinstitut für Schulqualität und Bildungsforschung (ISB) –\nLehrplanPLUS Gymnasium 12 – Informatik, grundlegendes Niveau<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nLesson reference: Inf12 LB1–3 · Represent graphs and explain shortest\nunweighted paths using breadth-first search. · Last accessed: 2026-11-22\n· Content verified: 2026-11-22<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
